\title{Infinitesimal Clopen Cuts in the Order Topology of the Surreal Line}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
The topology generated by open intervals on a linearly ordered set is its order topology. For a universe-relative surreal number field, this topology is governed by a strong non-Archimedean phenomenon: below every positive scale $d$ there exists $\varepsilon>0$ such that $n\varepsilon<d$ for every natural number $n$. We use this infinitesimal to construct, between any two distinct surreal numbers, an additive finite-step ray that is both open and closed. The surreal line is therefore totally separated, totally disconnected, and nonconnected. Every continuous map from a connected space into it is constant; consequently every path is constant and the space is not contractible. Nevertheless, its fundamental group at every basepoint is trivial because all loops are constant. We distinguish this mechanism from contractibility and discuss local compactness and the proper-class qualification.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The surreal numbers form a totally ordered field containing the real numbers, ordinal scales, infinitely large elements, infinitesimals, and iterated hierarchies of both. Their order is dense, which can suggest that the topology generated by intervals should resemble a connected continuum. This intuition is false.

On a linear order, bounded open intervals together with open rays generate precisely the order topology. The decisive difference between the real and surreal lines is the Archimedean property. Given a positive surreal gap $d$, one can choose $\varepsilon>0$ so small that no natural multiple $n\varepsilon$ reaches $d$. The region lying below some finite $\varepsilon$-threshold is then a nontrivial clopen lower set. Choosing $d$ as the distance between two points gives a clopen separator.

We develop this argument and its consequences. The proper class of all Conway surreals cannot be an ordinary set-based topological space, so throughout $\No_u$ denotes a universe-relative, set-sized surreal field. We omit $u$ when harmless.

\section{Definitions and setting}
\begin{definition}
For a linear order $L$, the \emph{order topology} is generated by the rays
\[
(-\infty,b)=\{x:x<b\},\qquad (a,\infty)=\{x:a<x\}.
\]
Their finite intersections include $(a,b)=\{x:a<x<b\}$. Conversely, intervals and the necessary rays generate the same topology.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A subset is \emph{clopen} if it is open and closed. A space is \emph{totally separated} if every pair $x\ne y$ admits a clopen set containing $x$ and excluding $y$. It is \emph{totally disconnected} if each connected component is a singleton.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A path from $x$ to $y$ is a continuous map $p:[0,1]\to X$ with $p(0)=x$ and $p(1)=y$. A contraction to $x_0$ is a continuous map $H:X\times[0,1]\to X$ satisfying $H(x,0)=x$ and $H(x,1)=x_0$.
\end{definition}

The universe qualification is substantive: boundedness of a family can require that its index type be small relative to the selected universe. The natural numbers satisfy this condition, which is all the main construction needs.

\section{Infinitesimal scales}
\begin{theorem}[Infinitesimal scale theorem]
For every $d\in\No$ with $d>0$, there exists $\varepsilon\in\No$ with $\varepsilon>0$ such that
\[
n\varepsilon<d
\]
for every $n\in\mathbb N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Construct a positive surreal $\delta$ below every positive dyadic scale,
\[
0<\delta<2^{-k}\qquad(k\in\mathbb N).
\]
It may be represented by a Conway cut with left option $0$ and right options $1,1/2,1/4,\ldots$. Put $\varepsilon=d\delta$. Given $n$, choose $k$ with $n<2^k$. Then
\[
n\varepsilon<2^k\varepsilon<2^kd2^{-k}=d.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This theorem fails in every Archimedean ordered field, where sufficiently many positive $\varepsilon$-steps exceed any fixed positive $d$.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Domination of natural scales]
There exists $M\in\No$ such that $n<M$ for every $n\in\mathbb N$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The natural-number image is a small family and has a surreal upper bound at the chosen universe level. Taking a surreal strictly above that bound gives the result.
\end{proof}

\section{Finite-step rays}
\begin{definition}
For $a,\varepsilon\in\No$, define the finite-step ray
\[
R(a,\varepsilon)=\{z\in\No:\exists n\in\mathbb N,\ z<a+n\varepsilon\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Openness]
The set $R(a,\varepsilon)$ is open.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It is the union of open lower rays
\[
R(a,\varepsilon)=\bigcup_{n\in\mathbb N}(-\infty,a+n\varepsilon).
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Closedness]
If $\varepsilon>0$, then $R(a,\varepsilon)$ is closed.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $z\notin R(a,\varepsilon)$. Thus $z\ge a+n\varepsilon$ for every $n$. If $z-\varepsilon<w$ and $w<a+n\varepsilon$, then
\[
z<w+\varepsilon<a+(n+1)\varepsilon,
\]
contradicting the choice of $z$. Hence an open neighborhood immediately below $z$ avoids $R(a,\varepsilon)$. Every point of the complement is interior, so the complement is open.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $\varepsilon>0$, the finite-step ray $R(a,\varepsilon)$ is clopen.
\end{corollary}

\section{Total separation}
\begin{theorem}[Clopen separation theorem]
For distinct $x,y\in\No$, there exists a clopen $U\subseteq\No$ such that $x\in U$ and $y\notin U$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $x<y$ and set $d=y-x$. Choose $\varepsilon>0$ such that $n\varepsilon<d$ for all $n$. Let $U=R(x,\varepsilon)$. It is clopen and contains $x$, since $x<x+\varepsilon$. If $y\in U$, then $y<x+n\varepsilon$ for some $n$, contradicting $n\varepsilon<y-x$. If $y<x$, perform the construction with the points reversed and take the complement.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Total separation and disconnection]
The order-topological surreal line is totally separated and totally disconnected. In particular, it is not connected.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Pairwise clopen separation gives total separation. If a connected subset contained distinct $x,y$, a clopen separator $U$ would split it into the disjoint nonempty relatively open sets $C\cap U$ and $C\cap U^c$, a contradiction. Since $0\ne1$, the whole space is not connected.
\end{proof}

Order density does not alter the argument. Density alone never implies connectedness; the rational line is a familiar example. In the surreal case, natural-number-indexed additive cuts remain bounded at larger non-Archimedean scales.

\section{Rigidity of continuous maps}
\begin{theorem}[Connected-source rigidity]
If $C$ is connected and $f:C\to\No$ is continuous, then $f$ is constant.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The image $f(C)$ is connected, while every connected subset of $\No$ is a singleton.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Path rigidity]
Every path in the order-topological surreal line is constant. Its path components are singletons, so it is not path connected.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The interval $[0,1]$ is connected; apply connected-source rigidity to the path map.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Noncontractibility]
The order-topological surreal line is not contractible.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A contraction to $x_0$ would make $t\mapsto H(x,t)$ a path from each $x$ to $x_0$. Path rigidity would force $x=x_0$ for all $x$, contradicting $0\ne1$.
\end{proof}

Thus any algebraic formula claiming to interpolate continuously from $x\ne0$ to $0$ must fail continuity in this topology. Endpoint identities do not establish topological continuity.

\begin{corollary}
Every continuous map from a connected sphere $S^n$, $n\ge1$, to $\No$ is constant.
\end{corollary}

\section{The fundamental group}
\begin{theorem}[Fundamental group theorem]
For every basepoint $x_0\in\No$,
\[
\pi_1(\No,x_0)=0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every loop is a path, hence constant. There is only the constant based-loop class.
\end{proof}

This triviality does not arise from contractibility. In a disk, loops can move and shrink; in the surreal line, nonconstant loops cannot move at all. Trivial fundamental group implies neither contractibility nor path connectedness without additional hypotheses.

\section{Algorithmic viewpoint and finite illustrations}
Given distinct $x,y$, a symbolic separator is obtained as follows: order the points; set $d=|y-x|$; choose $\varepsilon>0$ with $n\varepsilon<d$ for all natural $n$; form $R(\min(x,y),\varepsilon)$; and complement it if orientation requires. The construction is mathematical rather than a finite floating-point algorithm because selecting $\varepsilon$ invokes a surreal cut and the ray is a countable union.

Real arithmetic can illustrate only a finite window. Given $d>0$ and cutoff $N$, choose $\varepsilon=d/(N+1)$. Then $n\varepsilon<d$ for $0\le n\le N$, but $(N+1)\varepsilon=d$. This experiment pinpoints the Archimedean contrast: every positive real step eventually crosses the gap, whereas the surreal theorem chooses one infinitesimal that survives every natural index simultaneously.

\section{Local compactness and foundational scope}
An interval neighborhood basis is not a proof of local compactness. Local compactness requires compact neighborhoods. The results above do not characterize compact surreal intervals. Finite-step clopen cuts may help construct open covers with no finite subcover, but such an argument remains separate; total disconnectedness itself is compatible with compactness, as the Cantor set shows.

Likewise, the claims concern each universe-relative surreal field. A topology on the full proper class would require explicit definitions of open classes, class-indexed unions, continuity, and compactness. Set-level and class-level notions should not be silently identified.

\section{Further directions}
Connected-source rigidity suggests that all positive-dimensional homotopy groups vanish because maps from connected spheres are constant. Positive-dimensional singular simplices are likewise constant, suggesting vanishing reduced singular homology in positive degrees, while degree zero records the singleton components. Compactness of intervals and local compactness should be determined by explicit open-cover arguments. It is also natural to classify coarser topologies that permit continuous surreal-valued motion while retaining useful ordered-field structure. Finally, a genuine class-topological framework could test which universe-relative results persist for the full surreal class.

\section{Conclusion}
The interval-generated topology of the surreal line is its order topology. Below every positive gap lies an infinitesimal whose natural multiples never cross that gap. Finite-step rays formed from such infinitesimals are clopen and separate every pair of distinct points. The surreal line is therefore totally separated, totally disconnected, and nonconnected. Every connected-source map, path, and loop is constant. The space is not contractible, yet every fundamental group is trivial. The same arithmetic richness that supplies endlessly fine scales produces complete rigidity of connected motion.
\end{document}

